KNOWNBUG
main.ii
other.i --os macos --arch arm64 --no-library
^\[main\.assertion\.1\] line \d+ the C definition is called: SUCCESS$
^\[main\.assertion\.2\] line \d+ WRONG \(must FAIL\): FAILURE$
^\*\* 1 of \d+ failed
^EXIT=10$
^SIGNAL=0$
^VERIFICATION FAILED$
--
^warning: ignoring
--
On macOS the C front end renamed a function declared with the asm label
"_" + its name, Darwin's spelling of every C name in object files, to that
label, while the C++ front end, which does not apply asm labels, kept the
name.  The C++ call then had no body: Apple's <stdio.h> labels fopen this
way, so libc++'s basic_filebuf::open got an arbitrary FILE pointer from
fopen and closed it (cpp11_ofstream_from_string failed on macOS CI).  Such
a label is now taken to name the function it is attached to.  The files
are preprocessed (.ii, .i), so that no clang is needed on other hosts.
